## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-time propagation in three-valued logic, simulates realistic policy engine scenarios, and validates backtracking capabilities with multi-step constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Cross-temporal constraint satisfaction
- Backtracking with propagation and pruning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple two-period CSP checker with Kleene semantics
function kleeneNot(x) { if(x==='T') return 'F'; if(x==='F') return 'T'; return 'U'; }
function kleeneOr(a,b) { if(a==='T'||b==='T') return 'T'; if(a==='U'||b==='U') return 'U'; return 'F'; }
function kleeneAnd(a,b) { if(a==='F'||b==='F') return 'F'; if(a==='U'||b==='U') return 'U'; return 'T'; }
function kleeneImplies(a,b) { return kleeneOr(kleeneNot(a), b); }

// Check constraints across both periods
function validateConstraints(t0, t1) {
  const withinT0 = [
    kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t0.C1, t0.C2), t0.C3),
    kleeneOr(kleeneNot(t0.C5), t0.C4),
    kleeneImplies(kleeneAnd(t0.C2, kleeneNot(t0.C3)), kleeneOr(t0.C1, t0.C5))
  ];
  const withinT1 = [
    kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t1.C1, t1.C2), t1.C3),
    kleeneOr(kleeneNot(t1.C5), t1.C4),
    kleeneImplies(kleeneAnd(t1.C2, kleeneNot(t1.C3)), kleeneOr(t1.C1, t1.C5))
  ];
  const cross = kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t0.C1, t1.C4), t0.C2);

  return [...withinT0, ...withinT1, cross].every(v => v !== 'F');
}
```

Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design and reason about 3-valued constraint problems, including propagation and backtracking, in a realistic policy-like setting.

Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- Constraint propagation and backtracking search
- Minimal use of Unknowns to satisfy all constraints
- Concrete construction to validate satisfiability

Code Example
```javascript
function propagate3Valued(vars) {
  // vars: {Ingest:'T'|'F'|'U', Normalize:'T'|'F'|'U', Train:'T'|'F'|'U', Serve:'T'|'F'|'U'}
  // Simple forward-propagation stub for the three constraints
  // Returns updated map ensuring no explicit F is forced if possible
  // (This is illustrative; real impl would loop until fixed point)
  const v = { ...vars };
  // If (A ∨ B) → C and A is T or B is T, then C must be T
  if ((v.Ingest === 'T' || v.Normalize === 'T') && v.Train !== 'T') v.Train = 'T';
  // If (A ∨ B) → C and C is F, then both A and B must be F
  if (v.Train === 'F' && (v.Ingest !== 'F' || v.Normalize !== 'F')) { v.Ingest = 'F'; v.Normalize = 'F'; }
  // If (D ∨ E) → F and F is F, then D and E must be F
  if (v.Serve === 'F' && (v.Train !== 'F' || v.Ingest !== 'F')) { v.Train = 'F'; v.Ingest = 'F'; }
  return v;
}
```

